GARLIC is a program for calling runs of homozygosity in genotype data.  It implements the ROH calling method of Pemberton et al. AJHG (2012) and Blant et al. (2017).

Citations:

A Blant, et al. (2017) BMC genomics 18 (1), 928.
ZA Szpiech, et al. (2017) Bioinformatics 33 (13), 2059-2062.
TJ Pemberton, et al. (2012) AJHG, 91: 275–292

**NOTE TO WINDOWS USERS: In order to successfully run GARLIC you must have ann_figtree_version.dll and figtree.dll in the same folder as garlic.exe 

This ROH calling pipeline has four phases.

I.		Window-based LOD score calculation
II.		Kernel Density Estimation of LOD score distribution
III.		Assembly of ROH
IV.		Gaussian Mixture Modeling of ROH size distribution

Input files:

tped/tfam (required): 
	http://zzz.bwh.harvard.edu/plink/data.shtml#tr

map (required when --weighted or --cm are set):
	<chr> <snpid> <genetic pos> <physical pos>
	http://zzz.bwh.harvard.edu/plink/data.shtml#map 
	(negative positions will cause errors)

tgls (optional):
	<chr> <snpid> <unused> <physical pos> <GL ind1> <GL ind2> ... <GL indN>

	Per-genotype likelihoods should be given as either a phred-scaled (--gl-type PL) or a log10-scaled (--gl-type GL) representing the probability that the genotype is correct or a phred-scaled likelihood that the genotype is wrong (--gl-type GQ).  For example, if p = 0.999 is the probability that the genotype is correct, then PL = -10*log10(p) = 0.00434511774018, GL = log10(p) = -0.000434511774018, and GQ = -10*log10(1-p) = 30.

freq (optional, output during computation, can be generated without running whole pipeling with --freq-only): 
	CHR SNP POS ALLELE FREQ
	<chr> <locus ID> <allele> <freq>
	
centromere file (required if no --build is set):
	<chr> <centromere start> <centromere end>

GARLIC depends on zlib (http://zlib.net/) and POSIX threads.  On Linux and macOS both
come from the system, so there is nothing to obtain or vendor: the Makefile links -lz and
-pthread.  MinGW supplies neither, so win32 builds link libz.a and libpthreadGC2.a from
lib/win32, which are the only archives still included here.

GNU GSL and FIGTree were previously required and are no longer used.  FIGTree was replaced
by a direct Gaussian sum, which made the KDE reproducible; GSL was replaced by
src/garlic-math.cpp, which provides the ten routines garlic called and reproduces each of
them bit for bit, so results are unchanged.  That removed 44 MB of per-platform static
archives and vendored headers from the repository, and with them the main obstacle to
building on a cluster.

---CHANGES---
UNRELEASED - Correctness, performance, and command line work.

*** NOT BACKWARD COMPATIBLE: .roh.bed coordinates have moved by one base. *** BED
chromStart is 0-based and chromEnd is exclusive, but garlic wrote the 1-based position of
the ROH's first variant, so chromEnd minus chromStart came out one less than the length in
column 5 and a genome browser drew every ROH one base short. chromStart is now 0-based, so
chromEnd minus chromStart equals column 5. Coordinates from earlier versions are shifted by
one relative to these. The two count_features_in_roh.pl copies needed the complementary
change and now build their interval as ($start+1, $end); under the old convention they were
silently dropping the last base of every ROH. count_damaging_genotypes_in_roh.pl reads the
same file and carries the same one-line change, but that script has never been tracked in
this repository, so it is not distributed with it.

Wrong answers, produced silently and with exit 0:

- The genetic map was paired with the data by position in the file rather than by
  chromosome name, so a map whose chromosomes appear in a different order than the TPED
  applied the wrong chromosome's map to every site. A lexically ordered copy of the bundled
  example gave 3525 ROH where the correct answer is 5738.
- filterMonomorphicSites and filterMonomorphicAndOOBSites overwrote each locus name with
  its physical position.
- getMapInfo interpolated on uninitialised stack values when query positions were not in
  ascending order.
- A failed LOD cutoff search returned -1 and that value was used as the cutoff, producing a
  complete, plausible-looking .roh.bed.
- assembleROHWindows wrote one element past the end of its window array.
- --auto-winsize aborted with a use-after-free in subsetData, after writing output, so it
  looked like it had worked. This was the first command in example/example_README.txt.
- get_arg_max seeded its running maximum with numeric_limits<double>::min(), which is the
  smallest positive value rather than the most negative.
- KDE modes were located by testing floating-point equality against the density array.

Reproducibility. Automatic LOD cutoff selection is now deterministic: --kde-subsample
defaults to 0 (every individual), which removed the last consumer of random numbers on that
path, and the KDE evaluates its Gaussian sum directly instead of calling figtree, whose
clustering made results depend on more than the seed. Three runs that drew three different
seeds now select the same cutoff and call the same ROH. --seed remains for the paths that
still sample. The figtree and ANN archives are no longer linked.

Performance, on the bundled example (577,489 loci, 45 individuals), same output:
10.1 s to 1.6 s, and peak memory 377 MB to 287 MB. The macOS build was passing -fast, which
clang does not accept as an optimisation flag, so it had been compiling at -O0; one Makefile
now detects the platform instead of three being hand-edited. Pairwise LD is evaluated once
rather than once per window, the four possible per-locus LOD values are precomputed instead
of calling log10 per genotype, TPED parsing no longer goes through std::istream, bulk arrays
are allocated as one contiguous block with genotypes stored as one byte instead of two, and
LOD calculation and ROH assembly are threaded.

Command line. Exit codes are now 0 for success, 1 for a usage error, and 2 for a runtime
error; a misspelled flag used to exit 0, and argument errors exited 255. A rejected command
line no longer creates .log and .error files. New: --version, -h, --force (refuses to
overwrite existing calls without it), --seed, --quiet, --verbose, --chr, --outdir, --froh,
--load-params, --max-winsize, --kde-thin-step, --kde-points, --kde-cut, --mode-smooth-span,
--auto-winsize-threshold, --auto-winsize-coef, --auto-overlap-coef, --gmm-max-iter,
--gmm-tol. The last eight expose constants that were literals in the source and that each
move the selected cutoff or the size class boundaries; the two regression coefficient pairs
are empirical fits to human SNP-array data, which matters if you are not working on human
array data. --no-kde-thinning is now a deprecated alias for --kde-thin-step 1.

Every run writes <out>.params.json recording the effective value of every flag plus the
values the run settled on, including the drawn seed; --load-params replays it, with command
line flags taking precedence. --froh writes a per-individual autozygous total and fraction
by size class, with the denominator stated in the file header. The progress bar is drawn
only when stderr is a terminal, since it works by emitting backspaces. Size classes beyond
nine are no longer all drawn the same grey, and class labels no longer run past 'Z'.
--freq-only no longer requires a --winsize it never uses. Too few ROH for the size-class
GMM, and GSL domain errors inside it, are now reported instead of killing the process with
SIGABRT.

The command line reference in this file and in the manual is generated from the program's
own help strings by 'make docs' in src/. It had drifted to documenting 33 of 53 flags, and
described one flag the program has never accepted.

05JUN2020 - v1.1.6a Fixes a bug when using --auto-overlap-frac that can cause segmentation faults when not using chromosomes/contigs with numbers. ***I'm having trouble getting mscOS versions to compile, so the current binary for mscOS is still v1.6.0 and still has this bug.***

24AUG2017 - v1.1.6 Modified behavior of --cm flag based on user feedback. When this flag is set ROH lengths and length classifications will be reported in cM but ROH boundaries will be reported in bps.

18JUL2017 - v1.1.5 Introduced --ld-subsample argument. If set > 0, will use only a subset of individuals for computing LD statistics when calculating wLOD scores. 

18MAY2017 - v1.1.4 If a freq file is provided, GARLIC now ensures that internal coding of alleles is consistent with the allele specified in the file. Improved handling of loci with 100% missing data. Improved reading of gzipped freq files. Improved lod score cutoff discovery. Removed default setting for --winsize, which must now be specified. Now allows --auto-winsize to be used with --winsize-multi, will choose best from the list.

12MAY2017 - v1.1.3 adds a new option for --gl-type called GQ, which is a phred-scaled probability that the genotype is wrong. If p = 0.999 is the probability that the genotype is correct, then GQ = -10*log10(1-p) = 30.  The default for --gl-type is now set to none, so if you provide a TGLS file you will be required to choose between GQ, PL, and GL.

08MAY2017 - v1.1.2b fixes a bug that causes a crash when --overlap-frac is set to 0. Setting to zero now chooses the smallest sensible overlap fraction (1/winsize).  If you wish to let garlic automatically guess overlap fraction, use the new command line option: --auto-overlap-frac. Also includes an interation counter to monitor progress of GMM portion of the code.

07MAY2017 - v1.1.2 fixes a bug that causes a crash when a tgls file indicates a genotype has 0 likelihood. Add progress bars for LD calculations and LOD score calculations. Improve efficiency of tgls file loading.

06MAY2017 - v1.1.1a fixes a bug introduced with 1.1.1 that caused crashed when --resample is used.

05MAY2017 - Update to version 1.1.1. For some large datasets (e.g. WGS), KDE can take extremely long to complete, even when only a subset of individuals are considered.  Here we introduce some changes to speed this calculation up.  Instead of passing all LOD score windows to the KDE function, we now pass only non-overlapping windows.  This reduces the number of points sent to the function by a factor of WINSIZE. If you wish to retain all LOD scores, you may set the --no-kde-thinning flag. We also change the default number of random individuals for KDE (--kde-subsample) to 20, which still may be set to 0 to use all individuals. 

We also introduce a lower bound on the size of ROH reported.  Now, an ROH will not be called unless it contains at least WINSIZE*OVERLAP_FRAC number of SNPs. For example, for a WINSIZE (--winsize) of 100 and an OVERLAP_FRAC (--overlap-frac) of 0.25 the smallest possible ROH that will be reported will be 25 SNPs long.

Finally, if --overlap-frac is set to 0, GARLIC will attempt to guess a good choice.

02MAY2017 - Update to version 1.1.0a. Bug fixed that caused crashes when using TGLS file. 

28APR2017 - Update to version 1.1.0.  Includes several updates, including a method for reweighting LOD scores by gaps between SNPs, the option to report ROH lengths in cM, and the ability to provide per-genotype error rates.

25OCT2016 - Update to version 1.0.1.  This update fixes a bug where ROH that extended upto the end of the chromosome failed to get assembled and reported.
This update also introduces a new command-line flag --overlap-frac which is designed to reduce false positive calls by requiring a SNP be covered by at 
least OVERLAP_FRAC (default 0.25) proportion of high scoring windows to be included in an ROH call.  This helps to reduce false positive calls near the 
boundaries of true ROH.

garlic v1.1.6a -- a program to call runs of homozygosity in genetic data.
Source code and binaries can be found at <https://www.github.com/szpiech/garlic>.

Citations:

ZA Szpiech, et al. (2017) Bioinformatics, doi: 10.1093/bioinformatics/btx102.
TJ Pemberton, et al. (2012) AJHG, 91: 275–292.

----------Command Line Arguments----------

#BEGIN GENERATED FLAG REFERENCE -- regenerate with 'make docs' in src/
This section is generated by 'make docs' from the program's own help
strings. Do not edit by hand.

--M <int>: The expected number of meioses since a recent common ancestor for --weighted calculation.
	Default: 7

--auto-overlap-coef <double1> ... <doubleN>: <slope> <intercept>: coefficients of overlap% = slope*log(density) + intercept, used by --auto-overlap-frac. The defaults (6.375 63.888) are an empirical fit to human SNP-array data.
	Default: 6.375 63.888

--auto-overlap-frac <bool>: If set, GARLIC attempts to guess based on marker density.
	Default: false

--auto-winsize <bool>: If --weighted is set, guesses the best window size based on SNP density, otherwise initiates an ad hoc method for automatically selecting the # of SNPs in which to calculate LOD scores. Starts at the value specified by --winsize and increases by <step size> SNPs until finished.
	Default: false

--auto-winsize-coef <double1> ... <doubleN>: <slope> <intercept>: coefficients of winsize = slope*log(density) + intercept, used by --auto-winsize with --weighted. The defaults (8.3235 138.0521) are an empirical fit to human SNP-array data; override them for other ascertainment schemes or species.
	Default: 8.3235 138.0521

--auto-winsize-step <int>: Step size for automatic window selection algorithm.
	Default: 10

--auto-winsize-threshold <double>: Smoothness criterion at which the --auto-winsize search stops. Lower values demand a smoother LOD score density and therefore a larger window.
	Default: 0.5

--autosomes-only <bool>: Drop sex chromosomes (X, Y, 23, 24) before calling. A hemizygous male genotype is written as a homozygous call in a TPED and is indistinguishable from true autozygosity, so a male X chromosome is called as one run spanning the whole chromosome. garlic warns when sex chromosomes are present; this removes them. Not the default, because it would change results for anyone already analysing them deliberately.
	Default: false

--build <string>: Choose which genome build to use for centromere locations: hg18, hg19, hg38 or t2t-chm13 (T2T-CHM13v2.0, UCSC hs1).
	Default: none

--centromere <string>: Provide custom centromere boundaries. Format <chr> <start> <end>.
	Default: none

--chr <string1> ... <stringN>: Analyse only these chromosomes, e.g. --chr chr1 chr2 chrX. Names are matched after normalisation, so '1' and 'chr1' are the same. It is an error to name a chromosome that is not in the data.
	Default: all chromosomes in the input

--cm <bool>: Measure ROH lengths in genetic distance units. This requires a mapfile.
	Default: false

--dump-docs <string>: Write the command line reference to stdout in a documentation format and exit: txt for the README block, tex for a LaTeX description list. Used by 'make docs' so the documentation cannot drift from the program.
	Default: __none

--error <double>: The assumed genotyping error rate.
	Default: -1

--force <bool>: Overwrite existing output files. Without this, garlic refuses to clobber an existing <out>.roh.bed.
	Default: false

--freq-file <string>: A file specifying allele frequencies for each population for all variants. File format: CHR SNP POS ALLELE FREQ <chr> <locus ID> <allele> <freq> By default, this is calculated automatically from the provided data.
	Default: none

--freq-only <bool>: If set, calculates a freq file from provided data and then exits. Uses minimal RAM.
	Default: false

--froh <bool>: Also write <out>.froh.tsv: per-individual autozygous total and fraction, broken down by ROH size class. The denominator is stated in the file header.
	Default: false

--gl-type <string>: Specify the form of the genotype likelihood data: GQ, GL, or PL. GQ is a phred-scaled likelihood of the genotype being incorrect. PL is a phred-scaled likelihood of the genotype being correct. GL is a log10-scaled likelihood of the genotype being correct.
	Default: none

--gmm-max-iter <int>: Maximum EM iterations when fitting ROH size classes.
	Default: 1000

--gmm-tol <double>: Convergence tolerance for the ROH size-class EM fit.
	Default: 1e-05

--help <bool>: Prints this help dialog.
	Default: false

--kde-cut <double>: Extend the density grid this many bandwidths beyond the range of the data.
	Default: 3

--kde-points <int>: Number of grid points at which the LOD score density is evaluated. Also sets the resolution at which the between-mode minimum (the LOD cutoff) is located.
	Default: 512

--kde-subsample <int>: The number of individuals to randomly sample for LOD score KDE. If there are fewer individuals in the population all are used. The default, 0, uses every individual, which makes the selected LOD cutoff a deterministic function of the data alone. A positive value subsamples, which saves memory but makes the cutoff depend on --seed.
	Default: 0

--kde-thin-step <int>: Take every Nth LOD score window when building the KDE. 0 (the default) uses the window size, which keeps the sampled windows non-overlapping; 1 uses every window. Replaces --no-kde-thinning, which is equivalent to 1 and is kept as a deprecated alias.
	Default: 0

--ld-subsample <int>: The number of individuals to randomly sample for LD calculation during wLOD. If there are fewer individuals in the population all are used. Set <= 0 to use all individuals (will increase runtime).
	Default: 0

--load-params <string>: Read flag values from a <out>.params.json written by a previous run. Flags given on the command line take precedence over the file, so a run can be repeated with one parameter changed.
	Default: __none

--lod-cutoff <double>: For LOD based ROH calling, specify a single LOD score cutoff above which ROH are called in all populations.  By default, this is chosen automatically with KDE.
	Default: -999999

--map <string>: Provide a scaffold genetic map, sites that aren't present within this file are interpolated. Sites outside the bounds are filtered. This is required for wLOD calculations and any runs for which you wish to report ROH in units of cM.
	Default: none

--max-gap <int>: A LOD score window is not calculated if the gap (in bps) between two loci is greater than this value.
	Default: 200000

--max-winsize <int>: Upper bound on the window size that --auto-winsize will try. The search previously had no bound and would grow past the number of loci if the smoothness criterion was never met.
	Default: 1000

--mode-smooth-span <int>: Number of adjacent grid points used to smooth the density when locating its modes. Must be smaller than --kde-points.
	Default: 20

--mu <double>: Mutation rate per bp per generation for --weighted calculation.
	Default: 1e-09

--nclust <int>: Set number of clusters for GMM classification of ROH lengths.
	Default: 3

--no-centromere <bool>: Treat every chromosome as having no assembled gap, instead of requiring --build or --centromere. For organisms with no centromere gap in the assembly, and for references garlic has no built-in table for (only hg18, hg19 and hg38 are built in; T2T-CHM13 is not). Mutually exclusive with --build and --centromere.
	Default: false

--no-kde-thinning <bool>: Set this flag to send all LOD score data to KDE function. This may dramatically increase runtime.
	Default: false

--out <string>: The base name for all output files.
	Default: outfile

--outdir <string>: Write all output files into this directory, which is created if it does not exist. Equivalent to prefixing --out with the path.

--overlap-frac <double>: The minimum fraction of overlapping windows above the LOD cutoff required to begin constructing a run. ROH will have a lower bound size threshold of WINSIZE*OVERLAP_FRAC. If set to 0, GARLIC sets the value to the lowest sensible value: 1/winsize.
	Default: 0.25

--phased <bool>: Set if data are phased and you want to calculate r2 instead of hr2 while --weighted is set. Uses extra RAM. Has no effect on computations without --weighted.
	Default: false

--quiet <bool>: Suppress the progress bar and informational messages. Errors and warnings still go to stderr and to <out>.error.
	Default: false

--raw-lod <bool>: If set, LOD scores will be output to gzip compressed files.
	Default: false

--resample <int>: Number of resamples for estimating allele frequencies. When set to 0 (default), garlic will use allele frequencies as calculated from the data.
	Default: 0

--seed <int>: Random seed, for reproducible runs. Affects --kde-subsample, --ld-subsample and --resample. If 0 (default), a nondeterministic seed is drawn and written to the log file so the run can be repeated exactly.
	Default: 0

--size-bounds <double1> ... <doubleN>: Specify the size class boundaries ROH boundaries.  By default, this is chosen automatically with a 3-component GMM.  Must provide numbers in increasing order.
	Default: -1.000000

--tfam <string>: A tfam formatted file containing population and individual IDs.
	Default: none

--tgls <string>: A tgls file containing per-genotype likelihoods.
	Default: none

--threads <int>: The number of threads to use. Applies to LOD score calculation, the LD calculations under --weighted, the LOD score KDE, and ROH assembly.
	Default: 1

--tped <string>: A tped formatted file containing map and genotype information.
	Default: none

--tped-missing <char>: Single character missing data code for TPED files.
	Default: 0

--verbose <bool>: Show the progress bar even when stderr is not a terminal. By default it is drawn only on a terminal, because it works by emitting backspaces.
	Default: false

--version <bool>: Print the version and exit.
	Default: false

--weighted <bool>: Compute LOD scores weighted by LD and probability of mutation.
	Default: false

--winsize <int>: The window size in # of SNPs in which to calculate LOD scores.
	Default: 0

--winsize-multi <int1> ... <intN>: Provide several window sizes (in # of SNPs) to calculate LOD scores. LOD score KDEs for each window size will be output for inspection.
	Default: -1

-h <bool>: Prints this help dialog.
	Default: false

#END GENERATED FLAG REFERENCE
